##errorCatcher Echo
## Trailing windows ("last hour", "last 10 minutes", "last 24 hours") use
## $divumwx_last(seconds) from divumwx_cards.py, not WeeWX's $span(...): on the
## night the clocks go back, $span can put the start in the repeated hour, an
## hour late, and the whole file then fails ("start time is greater than stop
## time").
##
## DivumWX's extra fields (air density, cloud cover, pollen, VPD, aerosol,
## pm4_0, ...) are read through $divumwx_extras_binding (divumwx_cards.py):
## 'divumwx_extras_binding' when the station keeps them in DivumWX's own
## extras database (divumwx_extras.sdb), else 'wx_binding'.
##
## Fixed this session (beta report, Gert):
##  1. rainRateHourly chained .mm_per_hour onto .rain (group_rain, an
##     accumulated amount -- always mm, not a genuine rate type), which
##     threw KeyError: 'mm_per_hour' and crashed the ENTIRE template on
##     every generation cycle. Reverted to .mm, matching the alltime
##     "rainRate" key below it, which was already correct. hailRate-based
##     .mm_per_hour occurrences (prainRateHourly, prainRate) are genuinely
##     group_rainrate and are correct as-is -- left untouched.
##  2. This copy of the file had NO crash guards anywhere (an older/
##     reverted copy predating that convention -- see the handover doc's
##     "File source-of-truth discipline" note). A single unregistered
##     observation type (e.g. a particulate sensor field not on a given
##     station's schema) would silently revert the whole charts.json to
##     stale content with only a journal entry to show for it. Restored
##     per-field guards for every hardware-dependent/optional field:
##     particulates, air density, cloud cover, ET, greenhouse gases,
##     lightning, pollen, hail/prain, vpd, and solar/UV. barometer, base
##     rain, outTemp/dewpoint/humidity, and wind are genuinely universal
##     wview_extended schema types and are left unguarded.
##  3. IMPORTANT -- the guard pattern is NOT plain '#try / "key": $expr /
##     #except / "key": [] / #end try'. Cheetah writes literal template
##     text to the output stream as soon as it's reached, even inside a
##     #try block -- it does NOT buffer/roll back partial output if the
##     expression that follows then throws. '"key": ' is plain literal
##     text that gets written BEFORE $expr is evaluated, so on failure
##     the output was '"key": "key": [],' -- the literal prefix from the
##     failed attempt, immediately followed by the #except block's own
##     full line. That's a corrupted, duplicated key -- invalid JSON --
##     on every field that ever fails, which is exactly what silently
##     broke charts.json after the previous "#try / #except" fix in this
##     same file, even though it looked correct on manual inspection and
##     passed a hand-rolled (non-Cheetah) simulation. Caught only by
##     rendering through the real Cheetah engine with actual failing
##     fields, not by static analysis.
##     Fixed by computing into a #set variable first (#set never emits
##     literal output for a failing RHS -- it's a pure assignment
##     directive) and only emitting the literal '"key": $fv,' line
##     afterwards, once $fv is already a fully-evaluated, safe value:
##         #set $fv = []
##         #try
##         #set $fv = <expr>
##         #except
##         #pass
##         #end try
##         "key": $fv,
##     Verified against a real Cheetah3 render (not just JSON.loads on
##     hand-written text) with multiple fields deliberately failing at
##     once, both with and without hail data present.
##  4. Also found while verifying JSON well-formedness: the tail of this
##     file had one extra, unmatched closing '}' (3 '{' vs 4 '}' in the
##     original) -- silently produced invalid JSON on every successful
##     generation, independent of bugs #1-#3 above. Removed.
##
